WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.000
One day, little Eddie the Endorphin went to
play in the park with his receptors.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.540
But when he got there, he ran into the obnoxious
opioids - Hector the Heroin, Curtis the codeine,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.700
and Fred the Fentanyl.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.070
The obnoxious opioids punched Eddie in the
face and Eddie tried to fight back, but it

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.120
was too late, the obnoxious opioids had already
taken Eddie’s toy receptors and he was left

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.000
on the ground unable to fight back.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.570
Opioids are substances that inhibit the transmission
of pain information to the brain by turning

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.300
on the brain’s reward system.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.440
Endogenous opioids, such as endorphins and
enkephalins, are naturally produced by our

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.720
body to help modulate motivation, emotions,
food intake, and our response to pain.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.210
Like endogenous opioids, opioid drugs stimulate
opiate receptors at the synaptic membrane

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.350
and in some places, inside the nerve cells.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.110
Opioid receptors are part of a family of membrane
receptors called G-protein coupled receptors,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.500
or GPCRs.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:54.360
Ligands binding to GPCRs induces the phosphorylation
of intracellular GDP to GTP, this causes the

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.510
migration of a G-Protein to a membrane kinase
which cleaves a phosphate group off GTP, converting

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.470
it back to GDP, and using the extra phosphate
to initiate a cellular response.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.780
In opioid receptors, the cellular response
results in the opening of potassium channels

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.830
in neurons.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.170
This causes hyperpolarization, which limits
input from nerve fibers that control responses

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.810
from pain receptors.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.810
Once opioids cross the blood-brain barrier,
they bind to opioid receptors that are found

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.560
throughout the central and peripheral central
nervous system.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.350
This modulates the activity of the brain stem
which causes slowed breathing that results

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.670
in sleepiness and increased relaxation.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.470
Addiction and dependence can cause someone
to overdose resulting in forgetting to breathe

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.229
and eventually dying.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:37.869
Naloxone is a medication used to reverse an
opioid overdose.

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:42.570
It can be administered in a nasal spray or
via an injection up to 40 minutes after opioid

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.990
consumption.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:49.659
Naloxone separates the opioid molecules from
the receptors and binds to the receptors itself.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:54.280
In the future, improving access to treatment
and recovery services, and supporting research

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.539
on the role of opioids in pain and addiction
will be key in fighting the opioid crisis.

